<%!-- The one page the service worker is allowed to keep (issue #1729), shown
instead of the browser's own offline error.

It rides the **error layout** (`layout/error.html.heex`), which already solves
this exact problem for the rescued-500 path: a whole self-contained document
whose styling is inlined because it must not depend on `/assets/app.css`. A
stored page outlives the deploy it was stored in, so a reference to a digested
stylesheet would sooner or later point at a file that is no longer there — the
same reasoning, arrived at from the other direction. Borrowing it also means
one inline stylesheet to keep in step with Direction A instead of two.

Nothing here is per-member and nothing goes stale: no CSRF token, no session,
no counts. The operator's name and address are the one thing that is baked in
at cache time, which is the same staleness the whole document already carries
and a good deal fresher than the alternative of naming nobody.

**It must not claim the reader is offline.** A navigation fails here for
reasons this page cannot tell apart: no network, a captive portal, DNS, or our
own server being down — the browser reports the same nothing for all of them.
The old wording picked one ("until you are back online") and so was wrong
exactly when the fault was ours, which is when somebody most needs to know
where to write. The "Try again" button went with it: pressing it while there is
still no network re-serves this very page, which tells the reader nothing they
did not know a second ago. The brand wordmark above is the way back to the
site, and it works the moment there is a network again. --%>
<div class="error-page">
  <h1 class="error-page__title">{gettext("No connection")}</h1>
  <p class="error-page__hint">
    {gettext(
      "This page could not be loaded. Whether that is your connection or our server cannot be told from here. Please try again in a few minutes."
    )}
  </p>
  <ErrorHTML.operator_contact />
</div>
